-- title:
--   From L[f]-integrality to L[f⁻¹]-integrality after scaling
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ an $L$-algebra, let $f \in F$ be such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $L(f)$ generated by $f$, and let $u \in F$. Assume that $u$ lies in every valuation subring $V$ of $F$ that contains the image $\operatorname{algebraMap} L F(a)$ of every $a \in L$ and contains $f$. Then there is a natural number $m$ such that, for every valuation subring $V$ of $F$ containing the image of $L$ and with $f \notin V$, the element $u \cdot (f^m)^{-1}$ lies in $V$. Thus a single exponent $m$, depending on $u$ and $f$ but not on $V$, serves simultaneously for all valuation subrings of $F$ over $L$ which do not contain $f$; by the characterisation of integral closures as intersections of valuation subrings, the hypothesis says that $u$ is integral over the $L$-subalgebra generated by $f$, and the conclusion that $u \cdot (f^m)^{-1}$ is integral over the $L$-subalgebra generated by $f^{-1}$.
--
--   This is the "two-chart" integrality bound used when passing between the affine chart where $f$ is regular and the chart at the poles of $f$, where $f^{-1}$ is regular: an element integral on the first chart becomes integral on the second after rescaling by a fixed power of $f$. It is invoked in the treatment of regular prolongations and of Krull-dimension-one local behaviour on algebraic curves, for instance in [`AlgebraicCurve.RegularProlongation.exists_forall_mul_eq_sum_add_sum_inv_pow_mul_of_sum_finrank_eq`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.RegularProlongation.exists_forall_mul_eq_sum_add_sum_inv_pow_mul_of_sum_finrank_eq).
